# SYNOPSIS

```usage
drive script
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drive` reads <script>, a step a line, and does what it says as a user's hands would, then says what the desktop did.
The pointer goes to mouse(4)'s `mousein` as absolute records (`A x y buttons`), and keys to kbdfs(4)'s `kbin` as HID usages, where the pointer's and keyboard's drivers write theirs: Radharc (radharc(4)) and the programs above it can't tell the difference.
It needs `/svc/mouse`, `/svc/kbdfs` and `/svc/windows` in its namespace, as a cpu(8) session has them where the image has a desktop.

The steps, a line each; a blank line, or one whose first word starts with `#`, is none:

: `move` <x> <y>
  The pointer to (<x>, <y>), in the screen's pixels, the buttons as held.
: `press` [<button>], `release` [<button>]
  A button (`left`, `middle` or `right`; `left` if none) pressed or released where the pointer is.
: `click` [<button>] [<x> <y>], `rightclick` [<x> <y>]
  A move there if a place is given, 30 ms, the button pressed, 60 ms, and released.
: `key` <name> ...
  Each key pressed and released, 30 ms apart: a letter or digit, `f1` to `f12`, or `enter`, `esc`, `backspace`, `tab`, `space`, `up`, `down`, `left`, `right`, `home`, `end`, `pageup`, `pagedown`, `delete`, `ctrl`, `shift`, `alt` or `super`.
  Keys joined by `+` are a chord (`ctrl+w`): pressed in order, held 30 ms, and released in reverse.
: `type` <text>
  Each character typed, with shift where a US keyboard needs it: letters, digits, space and `-._/,`.
: `wait` <ms>
  A pause.
: `settle` [<quiet ms> [<limit ms>]]
  Waits for the input's first frame, up to two seconds, then until Radharc composes no frame for <quiet> ms (300 by default), at most <limit> ms after the last input (10000), then prints `drive: <line>: <ms> ms to the first frame, <ms> ms to the last, <n> frames;` and the processes that used CPU since the last `settle`, the most first, four at most (`cpu tracker 412 ms, windows 30 ms`), or `no CPU`.
  The times run from the last input to the first frame composed after it, and to the last: the first is how long the desktop took to answer; the last includes any other window's frames in the meantime (a console's line, a clock), so it is an upper bound.
  `, not settled in <limit> ms` follows the frames if frames were still coming.
: `waitfor` <title> [<limit ms>]
  Waits until a window with that title is in Radharc's `list`, at most <limit> ms (10000), and prints the time from the last input, or `not in <limit> ms`.
: `waitgone` <title> [<limit ms>]
  Waits until no window with that title is in Radharc's `list`, as `waitfor` does, or prints `still there after <limit> ms`.
: `mark` <text>
  Prints `drive: <text>`.

At the end it prints `drive: done, <n> steps in <ms> ms`.

# EXAMPLES

From the dev machine, the desktop's menu opened and closed on a board running the desktop image, with the script in the current directory:

```text
$ cat menu.drive
move 1400 800
rightclick
settle
key esc
settle
$ vx cpu -r . -c 'drive /mnt/term/root/menu.drive' 192.168.0.19
```

